# tests/platform.def -- container or community-platform definition template for rt.sh
#
# Usage:
#   1. Copy this file (keep the original as a reference) and edit the
#      values below for your own platform.
#   2. Tag the rt.conf (or -l <file>) COMPILE/RUN lines you want to run on
#      this platform with "+<PLATFORM_NAME>" in their Machines column,
#      using the same name given in PLATFORM_NAME below. "-<PLATFORM_NAME>"
#      excludes a line from this platform even if "+<PLATFORM_NAME>" is
#      also present.
#   3. Run:
#        ./rt.sh -a <account> -P myplatform.def -l rt.conf
#
# This file is a bash file of KEY=value assignments. rt.sh sources it in a
# subshell and reads back only the keys below; anything else is ignored.
# Quote values containing spaces. A list of tests is given in rt.conf. By
# default a community-platform run is sequential and skips baseline
# comparison: a portability check ("does the model build and run here at
# all?"), not a regression test. -c/-m opt into baseline creation/
# comparison; -r/-e opt into Rocoto/ecFlow (needs ROCOTO_SCHEDULER and/or
# WORKFLOW_MODULE_CMD). See the User's Guide chapter "Container and
# Community Platform Options" for the full field reference.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform and container
#
#   PLATFORM_NAME  Required. Becomes MACHINE_ID for this run, and the name
#                  used in the "+<name>"/"-<name>" rt.conf tag above. May
#                  be "container" to reuse the repository's existing
#                  container job-card templates and modulefile as-is.
#   RT_COMPILER    Required. This platform's one and only compiler:
#                  "intel" or "gnu". rt.conf lines tagged for this
#                  platform but built with the other compiler are skipped
#                  with a notice, not an error.
#   CONTAINER_IMG  Absolute path to a Singularity/Apptainer image (*.sif)
#                  on this host. Leave blank to build/run against a
#                  native software stack instead -- uses
#                  modulefiles/ufs_<PLATFORM_NAME>.<compiler>.lua
#                  that may need to be adapted for user's platform.
#   BIND_DIRS      Comma-separated host directories to bind-mount into the
#                  container (must cover the source tree, input data, and
#                  RUNDIR_ROOT below). Ignored for a native stack.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
PLATFORM_NAME=container
RT_COMPILER=intel
CONTAINER_IMG=/path/to/containers/rocky9-oneapi2024.2-ss192.sif
BIND_DIRS='/path1,/path2,/path3'

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler
#
#   TPN         Required. Tasks-Per-Node: MPI tasks per node on this
#               platform, a numeric entry (e.g., 48 on Stampede3).
#   SCHEDULER   Required. "slurm", "pbs", or "none" for an
#               interactive/no-scheduler run.
#   PARTITION   Slurm partition name (blank for PBS or no scheduler).
#   QUEUE       Slurm QOS / PBS queue name (blank for no scheduler).
#   MPI_LAUNCH  MPI launch command used when SCHEDULER is "none" and in
#               PBS job cards: "mpirun" or "mpiexec" (defaults to
#               "mpirun" if blank).
#
#   The scheduler account/project is set only with rt.sh's own -a <account>
#   on the command line.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
TPN=192
SCHEDULER=slurm
PARTITION=mypartition
QUEUE=myqueue
MPI_LAUNCH=mpirun

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run directory
#
#   RUNDIR_ROOT  Required. Top-level directory where compile and test run
#                directories are created. Should be a user-writable path,
#                preferably on a scratch or work filesystem.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUNDIR_ROOT=/path/to/runtime_dir

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Input data
#
#   INPUTDATA_ROOT       Required. Staged input-data directory (fix files
#                        and other RT input data).
#   INPUTDATA_ROOT_WW3   Optional. Defaults to a WW3_input_data_* sibling
#                        directory under INPUTDATA_ROOT if left blank.
#   INPUTDATA_LM4        Optional. Defaults to an LM4_input_data
#                        subdirectory of INPUTDATA_ROOT if left blank.
#   INPUTDATA_GFSv17opn  Optional. Only needed by tests that explicitly
#                        require GFSv17 operational input data.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
INPUTDATA_ROOT=/path/to/input-data
INPUTDATA_ROOT_WW3=
INPUTDATA_LM4=
INPUTDATA_GFSv17opn=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workflow manager (optional; only needed to use -r or -e)
#
#   ROCOTO_SCHEDULER    Rocoto's own scheduler-driver name -- not always the
#                       same as SCHEDULER above (e.g. plain PBS is "pbs" but
#                       PBS Professional is "pbspro"). Required to use -r.
#   WORKFLOW_MODULE_CMD A shell command run to put rocoto's or ecflow's
#                       command-line tools on PATH (e.g. "module load
#                       rocoto/1.3.7"). Leave blank if already on PATH.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
ROCOTO_SCHEDULER=
WORKFLOW_MODULE_CMD=
#ROCOTO_SCHEDULER=slurm
#WORKFLOW_MODULE_CMD='module load rocoto/1.3.7'
